<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prince Gong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1833">1833</a>–<a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Yixin, Prince Kung</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yixin, Prince Gong, was a Qing imperial prince and statesman, the sixth son of the Daoguang Emperor and a leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement</a>. Left behind with full powers when the Xianfeng Emperor fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1860, he signed the Convention of Peking with the British, French and Russians. In 1861 he set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zongli-yamen">Zongli Yamen</a>, in effect a foreign ministry, and joined the Empresses Dowager Ci'an and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Cixi</a> in a coup against the late emperor's regents, serving as prince-regent until 1865. Cixi dismissed him in 1884 and recalled him in 1894.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-gong.jpg" alt="Prince Gong" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Felice Beato · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Felice_Beato_(British,_born_Italy_-_Portrait_of_Prince_Kung,_Brother_of_the_Emperor_of_China,_Who_Signed_the_Treaty_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
